WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.413
JSON Schema: a vocabulary for describing data.

2
00:00:03.693 --> 00:00:08.024
JSON Schema is a standard for describing what valid JSON looks like.

3
00:00:08.304 --> 00:00:18.267
The current version is Draft 2020-12, and its validation vocabulary is a short list of keywords that you’ll see in every model provider’s docs.

4
00:00:18.547 --> 00:00:20.936
A schema is itself a JSON object.

5
00:00:21.216 --> 00:00:27.147
The keywords you’ll use most: type: string, number, integer, boolean, object, array, or null.

6
00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:30.733
properties: for an object, a schema for each named key.

7
00:00:31.013 --> 00:00:33.787
required: the list of keys that must be present.

8
00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:35.965
Keys not listed may be absent.

9
00:00:36.245 --> 00:00:38.763
enum: the value must be one of a fixed list.

10
00:00:39.043 --> 00:00:42.797
additionalProperties: whether keys not named in properties are allowed.

11
00:00:43.077 --> 00:00:45.531
Setting it to false makes the object closed.

12
00:00:45.811 --> 00:00:49.331
items: for an array, the schema every element must match.

13
00:00:49.611 --> 00:00:52.683
format: a named format such as date or email.

14
00:00:52.963 --> 00:00:55.011
description: human-readable text.

15
00:00:55.291 --> 00:00:58.768
It doesn’t constrain anything, but as you’ll see, models read it.

16
00:00:59.048 --> 00:01:00.691
Two details trip people up.

17
00:01:00.971 --> 00:01:11.488
First, required and nullability are independent: a key can be required but allowed to be null (the type is a union with null), or optional but non-null when present.

18
00:01:11.768 --> 00:01:19.555
Second, by default JSON Schema objects are open: extra keys pass validation unless additionalProperties is false.

19
00:01:19.835 --> 00:01:27.813
Closed objects are what you want for model output, because a stray assignee key usually means the model misunderstood the contract.

20
00:01:28.093 --> 00:01:30.333
You’ll rarely write these schemas by hand.

21
00:01:30.613 --> 00:01:32.213
You’ll generate them from code.
